 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 14,
   "text": "KELHAM ISLAND. A man-made island between the river and a channel called the goit, once crammed with works that rolled steel and ground knives. At its peak, Sheffield was the steel capital of the world."
  },
  {
   "x": 9,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "STAINLESS STEEL. Harry Brearley, in Sheffield in 1913, found that adding chromium made steel that would not rust. He was meant to be making better gun barrels. He made better forks instead."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "THE PEACE GARDENS. Cobbles and fountains by the Town Hall, with jets that shoot up in patterns and children who run through them in other patterns. On a hot day the whole city is here, shoes off."
  },
  {
   "x": 43,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "SHEFFIELD TOWN HALL. Opened in 1897, built of local sandstone, with a tower that has a statue of Vulcan on top, god of the forge. He is looking at the city, which is looking back."
  },
  {
   "x": 62,
   "y": 36,
   "text": "THE CRUCIBLE. A theatre that hosts the World Snooker Championship every spring, when the whole of Sheffield goes quiet and holds its breath for the black ball. A quiet Sheffield is the most unusual thing in England."
  },
  {
   "x": 72,
   "y": 36,
   "text": "THE CUTLERS' HALL. Home to the Company of Cutlers in Hallamshire, founded in 1624. Their annual feast is a grand affair, and the one dinner where everyone admires the cutlery."
  },
  {
   "x": 48,
   "y": 49,
   "text": "ENDCLIFFE PARK. Sheffield is said to have more trees per person than any other city in Europe, and a good number are in parks like this one. People here are rather proud of it, and rather polite about it."
  },
  {
   "x": 11,
   "y": 47,
   "text": "THE HILLS. Sheffield, like Rome, is built on seven hills, which is why every street is either up or down. Walking here is a free gym membership and a hearty cardiovascular argument."
  },
  {
   "x": 78,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "Road to Rotherham closed for resurfacing. Again. Sorry for any inconvenience."
  },
  {
   "x": 16,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: Harry Brearley, metallurgist, invented stainless steel in Sheffield in 1913.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "sheffield_brearley",
   "title": "Harry Brearley"
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 19,
   "plaque": "sheffield_name",
   "title": "Name: Sheffield",
   "text": "From Old English: the 'feld', or open land, beside the River Sheaf. The Sheaf may be named from an old word for a boundary, and it is said to have marked the edge of Mercia and Northumbria. Today it mostly runs in culverts under the city, minding its own business."
  },
  {
   "x": 60,
   "y": 36,
   "plaque": "sheffield_huntsman",
   "title": "Huntsman's Crucible",
   "text": "In the 1740s Benjamin Huntsman, a clockmaker unhappy with his springs, worked out how to melt steel in small clay pots called crucibles. The steel came out far more even than before, and Sheffield's name for quality followed. The theatre is named after those pots. It holds rather more people."
  },
  {
   "x": 65,
   "y": 8,
   "plaque": "sheffield_park_hill",
   "title": "Park Hill",
   "text": "Park Hill flats opened in 1961 with decks so wide they were called 'streets in the sky', and the milk float did its rounds up top. The estate was listed Grade II* in 1998, which surprised a few people. A graffiti proposal on one bridge, 'I love you will u marry me', now glows in neon."
  },
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 35,
   "plaque": "sheffield_hendersons",
   "title": "Henderson's Relish",
   "text": "Henderson's Relish, made in Sheffield since the 1880s, is a thin, spicy brown sauce in an orange label. It is not Worcestershire sauce, and you should never say so here. Locals shake it on pies, chips and stews, and some pack a bottle for their holidays, just in case."
  }
 ],
